Use this as the authoritative view of orchestration runs and their agent trees. Without `run_id`, inspect the grouped runs and select the one matching the work you intend to coordinate. Pass that run's exact `run_id` when listing a single tree or when calling `send_message_to_agent`, `wait_for_agents`, or `update_orchestration_goal`; never infer that two workers with the same name belong to the same run. Inspect worker paths, lifecycle states, verification status, wait reasons, latest output, and queued messages. Treat latest output as evidence to verify, not as proof. Use the canonical path returned for that run; do not duplicate the original task. A queued message is not completion evidence. Use `wait_for_agents` for an explicit checkpoint or selected subset. Do not build a polling loop after a background run: the runtime parks and wakes the parent automatically when the tracked worker runs reach terminal states.
